// The levels a learner must clear. An English speaker climbs all three in
// order (English, then Italian with hints, then Italiano), so the work left is
// what is still uncleared at EACH of them, summed. An Italian-browser user
// sees only Italiano, so one level. The ladder does not shorten the climb for
// someone going in order; it only stops double-charging someone who moves up
// before finishing a level.
function countedLevels() { return browserPrefersItalian() ? [EXAM_LEVEL] : [1, 2, 3]; }

// Uncleared active statements at one level.
function levelRemaining(lvl) {
  const p = loadProgressForLevel(lvl);
  let n = 0;
  for (const q of questions) {
    for (const s of activeStatements(q.id)) if (!isStmtMastered(p[q.id]?.st?.[s.number])) n++;
  }
  return n;
}
// Total clears still owed across the counted levels. Weak spots are not
// added: the drill belongs to the reserve days held back at the end, and the
// rate and the estimate measure only progress through the levels.
function remainingAcrossLevels() {
  return countedLevels().reduce((n, lvl) => n + levelRemaining(lvl), 0);
}
// ── Rate, and the date the work would be done ─────────────────────────
// There is no target and no deadline. The app measures what the learner is
// actually doing and says where that leads, so a quiet day changes an estimate
// instead of failing a quota.
//
// The rate is clears per CALENDAR day, rest days included, because a rest day
// is part of how long the material really takes. The window covers COMPLETED
// days only, from the oldest studied day within RATE_WINDOW_DAYS through the
// most recent studied day before today. Today is left out at both ends, for
// one reason stated two ways: a partial day is not a day. Before any work it
// would be a zero day dragging the average down; part way through it is still
// short of whatever the learner will finish with, so it drags the average down
// then too, which is how a learner who had answered 166 by breakfast watched
// the date move a day further out than it had been the night before. Today's
// effort is not ignored, it just reaches the estimate through the work
// remaining rather than through the rate: clears counted today shorten the
// remaining pile at once, so the date can only come closer as the day goes on,
// and the day settles into the average at midnight, whole.
const RATE_WINDOW_DAYS   = 14;
// Days set aside at the end, after the material itself is finished, because
// ready means ready to SIT the exam rather than ready at the last cleared
// question. Two parts, each derived rather than guessed.
//
// The weak-spot drill comes from drillEffort(): the clean answers each listed
// question still owes, one or two by drillCleanRun, summed and taken at the
// learner's own pace. It is a floor, and early on it is also
// incomplete, since the list is still gathering misses from levels not yet
// worked. That is deliberate: the estimate describes what is known today, the
// same rule the rate follows, and the date slides later as the list grows
// rather than pretending to know the end from the start.
//
// The mocks are a flat allowance. Five is the floor, since the readiness ring
// averages the last five, a learner short of 93% drills and retries, and two a
// day is right for a 20-minute exam whose misses want reading afterwards.
// Five to eight attempts at two a day is three days.
const MOCK_EXAM_DAYS = 3;
