// ── Playing through the silent switch (iOS) ───────────────────────────
// An iPhone on silent mutes Web Audio, which is exactly how most phones are
// carried, and Safari has no Vibration API to fall back on. The Audio Session
// API is the one supported way out: navigator.audioSession.type tells the
// platform what KIND of audio this is, and the platform decides accordingly.
//
// This is OFF by default and always will be. Someone who silenced their phone
// meant it, and an app that overrides that on its own is an app people delete.
// As an explicit setting it is a different thing: the owner of the phone is
// choosing it, for this one app, having been told what it does.
//
// Type choice matters, and the first choice here was WRONG. 'transient' was
// picked from the spec's prose, which describes it as a notification ping that
// plays over other audio, on the assumption that a ping would not be silenced.
// WebKit maps it to AVAudioSessionCategoryAmbient (DOMAudioSession.cpp,
// fromDOMAudioSessionType), and Ambient is silenced by the switch, so the
// setting did nothing it promised from the day it shipped. transient-solo is
// SoloAmbient and is silenced too. The ONLY non-recording type that survives
// the switch is 'playback' (AVAudioSessionCategoryPlayback), so that is what
// the setting now uses. Verify against that function before changing this,
// rather than against the spec's descriptions, which are about mixing.
//
// 'playback' is an EXCLUSIVE type: while our audio is active it pauses what
// the person was listening to. That cost is why this stays opt-in and why the
// category is released while the app is in the background (see ttsHush), so
// their music is theirs again the moment they leave. Safari's default is
// 'auto', which resolves to ambient; turning the setting off restores that.
